## The form

(Page note: the form takes the left two thirds of the page; the worked examples sit beside it on the right. Each box has a short label above it and a one-line starter inside the label, never a placeholder.)

### Task 1 · Where I found it

- Identify the source you learned the relationship from, by title, link or person. On Path A, this is the trail. On Path C, describe what you built and in which tool.
- Identify the part of the source that gave you the relationship (a section, a stop, a part of your build).

   **Where I found it.** The source, and the part of it that gave me the relationship.

### Task 2 · My relationship

- State the quantitative relationship in words.
- Write it as a math formula.
- Identify every quantity in the formula and its unit.

   **My relationship.** In words, then as a formula, with each quantity and its unit.

### Task 3 · My condition

State in one sentence what has to be true in a physical situation for your formula to apply. Write it so that you can check it against any new situation; in the next item you will use it to find situations where it fails.

   **My condition.** What must be true in the situation for my relationship to apply.

### Task 4 · My check

Run one check yourself, and report it in four parts.

- **Before.** Write the setup and the number your formula predicts, with its unit, before you run anything.
- **What I did.** Describe what you ran or measured.
- **What I got.** The number you got, with its unit.
- **What I make of it.** Compare the two numbers. If they differ, identify which part of your condition, or which measurement, could account for the difference.

   **My check · Before.** The setup, and my predicted number with its unit.
   **My check · What I did.** What I ran or measured.
   **My check · What I got.** The result, with its unit.
   **My check · What I make of it.** How close the two are, and what could account for any difference.

